WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.485 --> 00:00:04.652
(heart beating faster and faster)

00:00:16.066 --> 00:00:17.749
(light piano music)

00:00:17.749 --> 00:00:19.990
- [Announcer] Mankind and the elements.

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:22.799
For some, it's an uncomfortable bond.

00:00:22.799 --> 00:00:24.843
- A tropical rain forest is unforgiving.

00:00:24.843 --> 00:00:26.631
You can die in this kinda elements.

00:00:26.631 --> 00:00:28.988
- [Announcer] For others,
when weather strikes,

00:00:28.988 --> 00:00:31.031
inspiration begins.

00:00:31.031 --> 00:00:33.759
- We can start to kind of bring together

00:00:33.759 --> 00:00:35.477
geometry and nature.

00:00:35.477 --> 00:00:38.310
- [Announcer] These are the
people who challenge nature.

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:40.279
Seek out it's limits.

00:00:40.279 --> 00:00:41.946
Reveal it's secrets,

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:46.402
and embrace it's awesome power.

00:00:46.402 --> 00:00:47.621
In this episode,

00:00:47.621 --> 00:00:49.095
we'll meet an artist

00:00:49.095 --> 00:00:51.916
who transforms toxic
sludge from the Ohio River

00:00:51.916 --> 00:00:54.192
into works of art.

00:00:54.192 --> 00:00:55.411
- Nature has decided

00:00:55.411 --> 00:00:58.464
it would have a choice
in what would show up.

00:00:58.464 --> 00:01:00.798
- [Announcer] A scientist
working to save the Joshua tree

00:01:00.798 --> 00:01:02.389
from warming climates.

00:01:02.389 --> 00:01:04.467
- I'm really quite shocked, honestly,

00:01:04.467 --> 00:01:07.242
to see how many small trees there are.

00:01:07.242 --> 00:01:09.076
- [Announcer] And a
passionate boar hunter,

00:01:09.076 --> 00:01:12.327
preserving tradition in
the wettest place on Earth.

00:01:12.327 --> 00:01:15.508
- Wild boar hunting, it's not
a trophy sport in our family,

00:01:15.508 --> 00:01:17.540
it's a way to put food on the table.

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:20.071
- [Announcer] These pioneers
of the great outdoors ahead,

00:01:20.071 --> 00:01:21.571
on That's Amazing.

00:01:23.714 --> 00:01:26.714
(light piano music)

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:32.269
(fast, energetic music)

00:01:32.269 --> 00:01:33.535
- When you High Line often,

00:01:33.535 --> 00:01:38.063
you really learn to train
your fear like a muscle.

00:01:38.063 --> 00:01:40.919
Sometimes the height
isn't the scariest part,

00:01:40.919 --> 00:01:42.997
sometimes it's the exposure,

00:01:42.997 --> 00:01:47.164
which is how much space you
can perceive around you.

00:01:48.161 --> 00:01:51.494
(fast, energetic music)

00:01:52.501 --> 00:01:57.145
My name is Faith Dickey and
I'm a Professional Slackliner.

00:01:57.145 --> 00:02:00.616
(twangy guitar music)

00:02:00.616 --> 00:02:02.532
So Slacklining is the umbrella term

00:02:02.532 --> 00:02:03.751
for all the different facets

00:02:03.751 --> 00:02:06.688
of walking on a flat, woven band.

00:02:06.688 --> 00:02:08.929
One of these facets is Highlining,

00:02:08.929 --> 00:02:13.329
which is walking a slackline,
high off the ground.

00:02:13.329 --> 00:02:15.779
One of the best ways to
train for Highlining

00:02:15.779 --> 00:02:18.310
is setting up slacklines over water.

00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:20.388
When the current is
passing underneath you,

00:02:20.388 --> 00:02:23.650
you automatically start to
fall that same direction

00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:27.157
and it's almost like you're
not in control of your body.

00:02:27.157 --> 00:02:29.200
And that's great practice for Highlining,

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:30.175
because in Highlining,

00:02:30.175 --> 00:02:32.671
when you're dealing with
all the fear and adrenaline,

00:02:32.671 --> 00:02:34.950
often times it feels like
you're not in control

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:37.813
of your own body either. (splashing)

00:02:37.813 --> 00:02:41.830
(light, energetic music)

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:45.964
Chamonix is a town in the Alps of France.

00:02:45.964 --> 00:02:47.055
And it's one of the places

00:02:47.055 --> 00:02:50.138
that really drives me in Highlining.

00:02:51.175 --> 00:02:55.250
(light, energetic music)

00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:56.922
Being that high off the ground,

00:02:56.922 --> 00:02:58.187
you look at the valley below

00:02:58.187 --> 00:03:01.984
and the houses are just tiny
specks, they look like toys.

00:03:01.984 --> 00:03:03.484
It's unbelievable.

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:07.406
One of the High Lines that I
walked recently in Chamonix

00:03:07.406 --> 00:03:10.308
was on Aiguille Du Midi,
and when we reached the top

00:03:10.308 --> 00:03:13.036
it was a totally clear blue sky,

00:03:13.036 --> 00:03:15.068
and we were surrounded by peaks.

00:03:15.068 --> 00:03:16.543
But during the process of rigging,

00:03:16.543 --> 00:03:18.946
the clouds started to roll in.

00:03:18.946 --> 00:03:22.406
It was like an acid
trip, walking the line.

00:03:22.406 --> 00:03:24.449
I was out there in space and exposure,

00:03:24.449 --> 00:03:27.619
trying to balance and the
line was swinging beneath me.

00:03:27.619 --> 00:03:30.231
Meanwhile, these huge
white clouds were passing

00:03:30.231 --> 00:03:32.971
and the line would disappear,
the anchor would disappear,

00:03:32.971 --> 00:03:34.956
and then it would reappear.

00:03:34.956 --> 00:03:39.123
And there was no way to control
it, I just had to hang on.

00:03:41.469 --> 00:03:42.719
- Yes, yee-who.

00:03:44.335 --> 00:03:46.672
The beauty of the mountains is awesome,

00:03:46.672 --> 00:03:50.178
and yet it is a landscape that
demands focus and respect.

00:03:50.178 --> 00:03:51.397
Alpine Highlining

00:03:51.397 --> 00:03:56.114
is the purest form of
the sport I can think of.

00:03:56.114 --> 00:03:59.781
(vibrating, dramatic music)

00:04:03.554 --> 00:04:06.247
- [Announcer] In the
shadow of Waialeale, Kauai

00:04:06.247 --> 00:04:08.523
one of the wettest places on Earth,

00:04:08.523 --> 00:04:10.032
a fourth generation boar hunter

00:04:10.032 --> 00:04:13.422
passes the proud tradition to his sons.

00:04:13.422 --> 00:04:14.722
- When I get to take my sons out

00:04:14.722 --> 00:04:17.238
it becomes much more than a boar hunt,

00:04:17.238 --> 00:04:21.740
it becomes an adventurists'
environmental experience.

00:04:21.740 --> 00:04:23.601
- [Announcer] But new
threats to endangered birds

00:04:23.601 --> 00:04:26.411
are disrupting the harmony
of the fragile forests,

00:04:26.411 --> 00:04:28.327
where hunters find their game.

00:04:28.327 --> 00:04:29.847
- These are very, very endangered species.

00:04:29.847 --> 00:04:33.667
Really susceptible to a new
disease called Avian Malaria,

00:04:33.667 --> 00:04:37.669
and it's causing huge
declines in our populations.

00:04:37.669 --> 00:04:41.013
- [Sheri] These birds live
in that specific niche

00:04:41.013 --> 00:04:42.975
because that's what they need.

00:04:42.975 --> 00:04:44.431
And when that gets changed

00:04:44.431 --> 00:04:48.431
because of a pig rooting
where mosquitoes breed,

00:04:49.743 --> 00:04:51.856
you get a chain reaction of things

00:04:51.856 --> 00:04:56.828
that ultimately impact the
birds' ability to thrive.

00:04:56.828 --> 00:04:58.128
- [Announcer] So bird conservationists

00:04:58.128 --> 00:05:00.740
want to protect their habitats from boars,

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:02.610
and those who hunt them.

00:05:02.610 --> 00:05:04.815
- Hunters are the only natural predators

00:05:04.815 --> 00:05:06.894
and unless we can do our job,

00:05:06.894 --> 00:05:08.925
boars will continue to multiply

00:05:08.925 --> 00:05:11.538
and threaten our native birds.

00:05:11.538 --> 00:05:14.102
- [Announcer] There's a clash
playing out in paradise.

00:05:14.102 --> 00:05:15.322
- [Dr. Crampton] Nobody's right or wrong,

00:05:15.322 --> 00:05:19.489
frankly we're not gonna make
everybody happy all the time.

00:05:20.355 --> 00:05:24.022
(vibrating, dramatic music)

00:05:27.437 --> 00:05:31.222
(uptempo violin music)

00:05:31.222 --> 00:05:33.869
- Of all the US States,

00:05:33.869 --> 00:05:37.201
Hawaii has more endangered
plants and animals

00:05:37.201 --> 00:05:38.034
than any of them.

00:05:38.034 --> 00:05:41.338
And of all those in
Hawaii, Kauai has the most.

00:05:41.338 --> 00:05:42.171
- [Dr. Crampton] Waialeale,

00:05:42.171 --> 00:05:45.313
is the mountain in the center of Kauai.

00:05:45.313 --> 00:05:47.554
- [Billy] It's the
wettest spot in the world

00:05:47.554 --> 00:05:51.969
because it has 460+ inches of rain a year.

00:05:51.969 --> 00:05:55.735
- [Announcer] Waialeale means
Overflowing Water in Hawaiian.

00:05:55.735 --> 00:05:58.730
It's round summit is exposed on all sides,

00:05:58.730 --> 00:06:03.119
allowing for this unusual
amount of rainfall.

00:06:03.119 --> 00:06:03.952
- [Dr. Crampton] It's situated in a way

00:06:03.952 --> 00:06:05.925
as to receive the bulk of the rainfall

00:06:05.925 --> 00:06:09.060
that comes off the Pacific
Ocean, with the Trade Winds.

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:10.987
- We enjoy hunting in Waialeale.

00:06:10.987 --> 00:06:14.656
Home of over eight different
species of native birds.

00:06:14.656 --> 00:06:15.831
- [Announcer] Scientists estimate

00:06:15.831 --> 00:06:18.116
that the first birds to inhabit Hawaii

00:06:18.116 --> 00:06:20.194
arrived eight million years ago.

00:06:20.194 --> 00:06:21.100
- We think the wind

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:22.492
is what brought the forest birds,

00:06:22.492 --> 00:06:25.046
enough to actually start
a whole new population,

00:06:25.046 --> 00:06:28.794
and these birds exploded to
fill all of these different niches

00:06:28.794 --> 00:06:30.147
and habitats.

00:06:30.147 --> 00:06:31.169
It's like kids going off to college,

00:06:31.169 --> 00:06:32.550
they became their own thing.

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:34.838
- They were here, before Man.

00:06:34.838 --> 00:06:39.120
And because of Man, their
environments have shrunk,

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:42.453
and therefore their numbers have shrunk.

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:55.273
(fast violin music)

00:06:55.273 --> 00:06:57.595
- [Billy] The boar got here
over a thousand years ago,

00:06:57.595 --> 00:06:58.814
by the Polynesians.

00:06:58.814 --> 00:07:00.939
They brought it as a
food source on the canoe,

00:07:00.939 --> 00:07:02.645
to be sustainable.

00:07:02.645 --> 00:07:07.301
- When you bring in feral
animals into that environment

00:07:07.301 --> 00:07:08.473
they explode.

00:07:08.473 --> 00:07:11.283
That much pressure from an animal,

00:07:11.283 --> 00:07:12.386
has a huge impact

00:07:12.386 --> 00:07:15.439
on all of these birds
that don't have a defense.

00:07:15.439 --> 00:07:19.284
Frankly the only pressures
they have are hunting.

00:07:19.284 --> 00:07:20.117
- [Billy] Wild boar hunting,

00:07:20.117 --> 00:07:22.391
it's not a trophy sport in our family,

00:07:22.391 --> 00:07:24.353
it's a way to put food on the table,

00:07:24.353 --> 00:07:26.222
and help protect our forest,

00:07:26.222 --> 00:07:28.092
and I feel like I am doing justice

00:07:28.092 --> 00:07:29.810
when I am out there with the hunting dogs.

00:07:29.810 --> 00:07:32.260
I'm not only doin' a spiritual thing,

00:07:32.260 --> 00:07:34.222
forefathers did a thousand years ago,

00:07:34.222 --> 00:07:36.889
I'm bein' a steward of the land.

00:07:38.123 --> 00:07:42.326
The wild boar enjoys to
wallow onto the pond of mud,

00:07:42.326 --> 00:07:44.288
which becomes a mosquito bath.

00:07:44.288 --> 00:07:45.704
- When the birds came to Kauai,

00:07:45.704 --> 00:07:48.607
there were no mosquitoes, and
there were no avian diseases.

00:07:48.607 --> 00:07:51.625
The group of birds that's
most prevalent here in Hawaii

00:07:51.625 --> 00:07:56.269
is really susceptible to a new
disease called Avian Malaria,

00:07:56.269 --> 00:08:00.054
and it's causing huge
declines in our populations.

00:08:00.054 --> 00:08:03.120
99% of EEB die if they
are bitten just once

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:05.976
by an infected mosquito,
they're that susceptible.

00:08:05.976 --> 00:08:07.101
- [Billy] These wild boars

00:08:07.101 --> 00:08:09.238
are gonna eventually run the island.

00:08:09.238 --> 00:08:12.488
Hunters are the only natural predators.

00:08:14.751 --> 00:08:16.264
- [Announcer] Kauais' fragile forest

00:08:16.264 --> 00:08:18.681
could become a paradise lost.

00:08:21.314 --> 00:08:25.297
- Hawaii is a unique place
because of it's people.

00:08:25.297 --> 00:08:27.665
Because of the culture that lives here.

00:08:27.665 --> 00:08:30.359
If we don't take care,
our natural resources,

00:08:30.359 --> 00:08:34.526
it will be used up, and we
don't want that to happen.

00:08:35.711 --> 00:08:39.008
- These birds, live in
that specific niche,

00:08:39.008 --> 00:08:40.889
because that's what they need.

00:08:40.889 --> 00:08:42.352
And when that gets changed

00:08:42.352 --> 00:08:46.352
because of a pig rooting
where mosquitoes breed,

00:08:47.750 --> 00:08:49.875
you get a chain reaction of things

00:08:49.875 --> 00:08:54.229
that ultimately impact the
birds ability to thrive.

00:08:54.229 --> 00:08:55.606
- 99% of EEB die

00:08:55.606 --> 00:08:58.172
if they are bitten just once
by an infected mosquito.

00:08:58.172 --> 00:09:00.331
And if they go extinct here,
they're extinct everywhere.

00:09:00.331 --> 00:09:03.728
We wanna make sure that we're
managing all the threats.

00:09:03.728 --> 00:09:05.487
- Hunters are the only natural predators

00:09:05.487 --> 00:09:07.479
that boars have on the island.

00:09:07.479 --> 00:09:09.650
And unless we can do our job,

00:09:09.650 --> 00:09:11.601
boars will continue to multiply

00:09:11.601 --> 00:09:14.101
and threaten our native birds.

00:09:15.443 --> 00:09:18.706
(dramatic music)

00:09:18.706 --> 00:09:21.289
(dogs barking)

00:09:27.518 --> 00:09:30.130
Wild boar hunting is dangerous.

00:09:30.130 --> 00:09:33.256
There's no if, ands, or buts about it.

00:09:33.256 --> 00:09:34.214
Although there's more to hunting

00:09:34.214 --> 00:09:37.070
than just putting your
knife into a big old boar,

00:09:37.070 --> 00:09:38.614
it's a way of life,

00:09:38.614 --> 00:09:40.978
passed down from many
different generations.

00:09:40.978 --> 00:09:42.087
This is a trail knife

00:09:42.087 --> 00:09:44.669
given to me from my Great Uncle

00:09:44.669 --> 00:09:47.051
down to my Dad and then to me.

00:09:47.051 --> 00:09:48.549
When I get to take my sons out,

00:09:48.549 --> 00:09:50.964
it becomes much more than a boar hunt,

00:09:50.964 --> 00:09:54.633
it becomes an adventurists'
environmental experience.

00:09:54.633 --> 00:09:58.800
Provides food for the table,
it teaches them Malama Daina,

00:09:59.683 --> 00:10:03.461
which means Love The Land You Live On.

00:10:03.461 --> 00:10:06.211
(dramatic music)

00:10:07.148 --> 00:10:09.731
(dogs barking)

00:10:12.616 --> 00:10:14.567
We enjoy hunting in Waialeale.

00:10:14.567 --> 00:10:15.902
It's not the best place to hunt,

00:10:15.902 --> 00:10:18.758
it's miserable, it's raining, it's cold.

00:10:18.758 --> 00:10:20.918
We have to travel through
treacherous terrain

00:10:20.918 --> 00:10:22.380
before we even got to an area

00:10:22.380 --> 00:10:24.963
that was decent enough to hunt.

00:10:27.884 --> 00:10:30.449
Looks like some fresh
pig digging on our trail.

00:10:30.449 --> 00:10:33.706
You can tell by the lighter colored soil

00:10:33.706 --> 00:10:37.363
has gotten the rain compared
to the darker soil, ya see it?

00:10:37.363 --> 00:10:38.907
You want to look at the diggings,

00:10:38.907 --> 00:10:40.289
with the rain happening.

00:10:40.289 --> 00:10:42.611
You can actually track the animal.

00:10:42.611 --> 00:10:44.887
The dogs have a sense,
a good sense of smell,

00:10:44.887 --> 00:10:47.970
and they're on the track of the boar.

00:10:50.181 --> 00:10:52.848
(boar grunting)

00:10:55.266 --> 00:10:58.157
- You wanna listen to the boars' language.

00:10:58.157 --> 00:11:00.074
It's roar, it's scream.

00:11:01.338 --> 00:11:03.660
(dog barking)

00:11:03.660 --> 00:11:06.516
You wanna listen for the bark, of the dog.

00:11:06.516 --> 00:11:09.099
(dogs barking)

00:11:09.941 --> 00:11:12.391
You can tell whether the dogs
have the boar pinned or not.

00:11:12.391 --> 00:11:13.412
At that point,

00:11:13.412 --> 00:11:16.384
you have a fraction of
a second to do your job

00:11:16.384 --> 00:11:18.381
or that could mean the
life of your hunting dog,

00:11:18.381 --> 00:11:19.647
your kid.

00:11:19.647 --> 00:11:22.259
You gotta get in there quick as possible

00:11:22.259 --> 00:11:26.426
and take care of business before
business takes care of you.

00:11:28.270 --> 00:11:31.020
(dramatic music)

00:11:36.739 --> 00:11:40.362
(Hawaiian drum music)

00:11:40.362 --> 00:11:43.612
(background chatter,
dogs barking and cooking)

00:11:43.612 --> 00:11:45.366
There is a big sense of pride

00:11:45.366 --> 00:11:47.444
when you harvest food from the land

00:11:47.444 --> 00:11:48.918
and put it on your table.

00:11:48.918 --> 00:11:52.866
It makes you feel like
you can sustain life.

00:11:52.866 --> 00:11:56.743
(Hawaiian drum music)

00:11:56.743 --> 00:12:00.203
A luau happens on a weekly basis,

00:12:00.203 --> 00:12:02.897
with our immediate family and friends.

00:12:02.897 --> 00:12:05.660
It is our job to still practice

00:12:05.660 --> 00:12:08.388
what our forefathers have taught us.

00:12:08.388 --> 00:12:09.897
- The Division of Forestry and Wildlife

00:12:09.897 --> 00:12:12.370
both have to protect animals and plants,

00:12:12.370 --> 00:12:14.170
and the right to hunt.

00:12:14.170 --> 00:12:16.167
And we have to do both.

00:12:16.167 --> 00:12:19.429
- If pigs are contributing
to this mosquito lifecycle,

00:12:19.429 --> 00:12:21.217
then one of our most important tools

00:12:21.217 --> 00:12:22.726
is conservation fencing.

00:12:22.726 --> 00:12:25.710
So that the areas inside the fences

00:12:25.710 --> 00:12:29.019
can be a subject to pig removal

00:12:29.019 --> 00:12:30.691
and they will help us slowly but surely

00:12:30.691 --> 00:12:33.338
reduce those populations
from within the fences.

00:12:33.338 --> 00:12:35.904
And hopefully lessen the
amount of habitat out there

00:12:35.904 --> 00:12:37.947
that's available for
mosquitoes to breed in.

00:12:37.947 --> 00:12:41.035
All of these fences are
constructed with little gates

00:12:41.035 --> 00:12:44.635
that hunters can go in and out of.

00:12:44.635 --> 00:12:47.385
(dramatic music)

00:12:48.454 --> 00:12:51.275
- I would like to see
more, smaller enclosures,

00:12:51.275 --> 00:12:54.445
and have the boar still be able to roam

00:12:54.445 --> 00:12:57.220
and have their natural habitat.

00:12:57.220 --> 00:13:00.854
- A lot of hunters, they want
to be able to roam freely,

00:13:00.854 --> 00:13:03.419
and not have to go all
the way around the fence.

00:13:03.419 --> 00:13:06.357
- The Polynesians had
access to this island.

00:13:06.357 --> 00:13:09.201
All of a sudden now, we
have only a sliver left.

00:13:09.201 --> 00:13:12.301
Those are areas that
their forefathers went to,

00:13:12.301 --> 00:13:14.333
not only to harvest food,

00:13:14.333 --> 00:13:18.083
but to sing and dance
and praise to the AKUA.

00:13:19.418 --> 00:13:22.727
And now there's a gate,
or a stepping bridge

00:13:22.727 --> 00:13:24.724
that takes you over a fence.

00:13:24.724 --> 00:13:28.341
Can we work together to solve the problem,

00:13:28.341 --> 00:13:30.967
which is caring for the native birds?

00:13:30.967 --> 00:13:34.634
(Hawaiian prayer of Thanks)

00:13:39.949 --> 00:13:41.981
- There's a lot to be learned

00:13:41.981 --> 00:13:44.953
from people who come from
generations of knowledge.

00:13:44.953 --> 00:13:47.740
I would like to see our ways of life

00:13:47.740 --> 00:13:49.446
be part of the equation,

00:13:49.446 --> 00:13:51.908
to solve these environmental issues.

00:13:51.908 --> 00:13:53.324
And that's why it hurts

00:13:53.324 --> 00:13:56.377
when we work with these
people who are textbook smart.

00:13:56.377 --> 00:13:58.711
- We really try to work with hunters

00:13:58.711 --> 00:14:01.033
and use their expertise.

00:14:01.033 --> 00:14:03.274
The fact of the matter is,

00:14:03.274 --> 00:14:06.274
you can't save birds without fences.

00:14:07.918 --> 00:14:10.600
We're just trying to stop extinction.

00:14:10.600 --> 00:14:12.399
We're not succeeding.

00:14:12.399 --> 00:14:14.802
- So we lose these birds,

00:14:14.802 --> 00:14:16.927
we lose the function of the forest,

00:14:16.927 --> 00:14:19.005
we lose our connection to history.

00:14:19.005 --> 00:14:21.130
It's a battle that can't be lost, really.

00:14:21.130 --> 00:14:25.147
Because it would be just such a huge tragedy.

00:14:25.147 --> 00:14:29.025
- You wanna take something
back to the way it used to be,

00:14:29.025 --> 00:14:31.683
well at what point is appropriate?

00:14:31.683 --> 00:14:33.715
Before or after humans came?

00:14:33.715 --> 00:14:35.108
Which humans?

00:14:35.108 --> 00:14:37.059
Very, very debated topic

00:14:37.059 --> 00:14:39.741
throughout conservation in the world.

00:14:39.741 --> 00:14:41.819
There's definitely a partnership.

00:14:41.819 --> 00:14:45.940
It's a balance and
nobody's right or wrong.

00:14:45.940 --> 00:14:49.365
- There need to be a
bridge, a common element,

00:14:49.365 --> 00:14:53.532
and if I have to go out with
just giving my son one memory,

00:14:54.416 --> 00:14:58.317
It'll be the memory of
living a cultural lifestyle

00:14:58.317 --> 00:15:01.951
that is taught traditionally
by his forefathers.

00:15:01.951 --> 00:15:04.034
That's my gift to my son.

00:15:05.422 --> 00:15:09.160
Let's share out Manao, which
we Hawaiian say our knowledge,

00:15:09.160 --> 00:15:11.436
from our mind and our heart.

00:15:11.436 --> 00:15:13.433
Teach us the textbook stuff.

00:15:13.433 --> 00:15:16.850
Let's work together to solve the problem.

00:15:18.042 --> 00:15:19.505
- [Announcer] Conservationists
continue to fight

00:15:19.505 --> 00:15:21.873
the extinction of Kauais' native birds.

00:15:21.873 --> 00:15:24.623
With plans to erect more fencing.

00:15:26.355 --> 00:15:30.522
This vivid debate, over
conservation, culture, and history,

00:15:31.893 --> 00:15:36.060
continues to play out in
the Wettest Place on Earth.

00:15:37.315 --> 00:15:41.192
(dramatic violin music)

00:15:41.192 --> 00:15:42.730
- [Announcer] Across the Rust Belt,

00:15:42.730 --> 00:15:45.470
abandoned coal mines are leaching toxins

00:15:45.470 --> 00:15:48.117
into rivers and streams,

00:15:48.117 --> 00:15:50.927
turning them shades of dirty red.

00:15:50.927 --> 00:15:53.051
The color is disturbing.

00:15:53.051 --> 00:15:57.115
But for two men, it's also
the tint of inspiration.

00:15:57.115 --> 00:15:59.565
One is a civil engineer,

00:15:59.565 --> 00:16:03.361
with plans to extract these
toxins from the waters.

00:16:03.361 --> 00:16:05.892
The other is a painter,

00:16:05.892 --> 00:16:08.783
who transforms that material
into acrylic paints,

00:16:08.783 --> 00:16:11.035
and stunning works of art.

00:16:11.035 --> 00:16:15.157
(dramatic violin music)

00:16:15.157 --> 00:16:18.240
(gentle piano music)

00:16:19.650 --> 00:16:22.558
- This part of Southeastern
Ohio is considered Appalachia.

00:16:22.558 --> 00:16:25.326
And we have a strong
relationship with the Ohio River.

00:16:25.326 --> 00:16:27.114
We have a strong relationship with

00:16:27.114 --> 00:16:30.551
all of the environmental
destruction that's happened here.

00:16:30.551 --> 00:16:31.445
- [Announcer] The abandoned mines

00:16:31.445 --> 00:16:33.720
have irretrievably altered the watershed.

00:16:33.720 --> 00:16:36.994
Discharging acidic water into
nearby streams and rivers,

00:16:36.994 --> 00:16:40.129
due to a process known
as acid mine drainage.

00:16:40.129 --> 00:16:41.104
- So just in Ohio,

00:16:41.104 --> 00:16:43.600
there's more than 1,300 streamed miles

00:16:43.600 --> 00:16:45.435
that are impacted by acid mine drainage.

00:16:45.435 --> 00:16:46.781
The mine that discharges here

00:16:46.781 --> 00:16:48.894
at this specific treatment site,

00:16:48.894 --> 00:16:52.772
it's been abandoned for
probably about a hundred years.

00:16:52.772 --> 00:16:56.557
- Fish and other aquatic life
is really sensitive to Ph,

00:16:56.557 --> 00:16:59.773
and the streams get coated
with this iron sludge.

00:16:59.773 --> 00:17:03.384
- This is the mine that we
call the Bat Gate Mine Seep,

00:17:03.384 --> 00:17:05.880
and it had been pumping
out acid mine drainage

00:17:05.880 --> 00:17:07.668
for the last hundred years now.

00:17:07.668 --> 00:17:09.746
- [Announcer] The toxic
discharge at Bat Gate

00:17:09.746 --> 00:17:13.090
is the result of an abandoned,
improperly sealed coal mine.

00:17:13.090 --> 00:17:17.257
- It delivers about 4,000
pounds of iron every day,

00:17:18.140 --> 00:17:20.385
into this one stream nearby.

00:17:20.385 --> 00:17:25.256
That's like junking two cars
into the stream everyday.

00:17:25.256 --> 00:17:27.869
(bouncy xylophone music)

00:17:27.869 --> 00:17:29.332
What I'm doing in my research right now

00:17:29.332 --> 00:17:31.897
is trying to come up
with a way to clean up streams

00:17:31.897 --> 00:17:33.565
in Southeast Ohio.

00:17:33.565 --> 00:17:35.489
So through our research we've discovered

00:17:35.489 --> 00:17:39.402
that when we remove iron
from the acid mine drainage,

00:17:39.402 --> 00:17:43.140
we can precipitate it and
settle it as a pigment.

00:17:43.140 --> 00:17:45.137
And I realized that by producing pigment,

00:17:45.137 --> 00:17:47.459
I could pay for this whole process.

00:17:47.459 --> 00:17:49.862
When I got to the dry pigment,

00:17:49.862 --> 00:17:53.171
I realized that I didn't
have the skills to figure out

00:17:53.171 --> 00:17:56.921
what made a good pigment
and what made a bad pigment.

00:17:56.921 --> 00:17:59.650
I basically was knocking on
doors in the art department

00:17:59.650 --> 00:18:01.693
to see if I could find anyone to help me,

00:18:01.693 --> 00:18:03.237
and that's when I ran into John.

00:18:03.237 --> 00:18:04.070
I was like

00:18:04.070 --> 00:18:07.266
"Could you tell me if this
was a good paint or not."

00:18:07.266 --> 00:18:09.356
- And we sat down and he said
in very scientific jargon

00:18:09.356 --> 00:18:10.784
"Well, the pigment needs to be this,

00:18:10.784 --> 00:18:11.712
and it needs to be that."

00:18:11.712 --> 00:18:14.766
And I was like "Yeah, I can do
that, but what we really need

00:18:14.766 --> 00:18:16.809
to do is make something
that visually is compelling.”

00:18:16.809 --> 00:18:19.085
We need people to look
at this and go,"What?”

00:18:19.085 --> 00:18:20.269
"What are you doing?

00:18:20.269 --> 00:18:21.209
That's crazy!"

00:18:21.209 --> 00:18:22.219
and get on board.

00:18:22.219 --> 00:18:25.219
(snappy drum music)

00:18:26.585 --> 00:18:27.418
One of the things I really liked

00:18:27.418 --> 00:18:29.847
about getting the pigment
from these acid mine seeps

00:18:29.847 --> 00:18:31.682
is that it has characteristics

00:18:31.682 --> 00:18:33.841
of the place where you get it from.

00:18:33.841 --> 00:18:35.559
So from Guy, I would come back and say

00:18:35.559 --> 00:18:37.637
"Well, this is too orange, I
want it to be a deeper red

00:18:37.637 --> 00:18:40.377
because it's more valuable
for these reasons."

00:18:40.377 --> 00:18:41.922
And once I get the pigment from the lab,

00:18:41.922 --> 00:18:44.453
I put it on to a tempered glass table,

00:18:44.453 --> 00:18:46.531
I'll add an oil-dispersant for there,

00:18:46.531 --> 00:18:49.631
which basically makes the
pigment want to stick to things

00:18:49.631 --> 00:18:51.301
so it wants to become a good paint.

00:18:51.301 --> 00:18:53.460
And then that can be scraped
up in to jars or tubes

00:18:53.460 --> 00:18:54.772
and you can use oil paint

00:18:54.772 --> 00:18:58.801
for years and years and years and years.

00:18:58.801 --> 00:19:03.166
- He makes these connections
between nature, and pollution,

00:19:03.166 --> 00:19:04.838
and science,

00:19:04.838 --> 00:19:07.160
and he put's that in this something

00:19:07.160 --> 00:19:09.243
that is beautiful to see.

00:19:10.829 --> 00:19:12.094
- We've had recent interest

00:19:12.094 --> 00:19:14.486
from Gamblin Artist Colors in Portland,

00:19:14.486 --> 00:19:16.320
and we are producing a custom batch

00:19:16.320 --> 00:19:20.774
of the color, 500 tubes of oil
paint, with our name on it.

00:19:20.774 --> 00:19:22.004
The proceeds from selling the pigment

00:19:22.004 --> 00:19:24.605
will pay for the plant,
it will pay for employees,

00:19:24.605 --> 00:19:27.786
it'll pay for the cleanup of a stream.

00:19:27.786 --> 00:19:31.327
The moment that the plant becomes viable,

00:19:31.327 --> 00:19:33.696
that is the moment that
the stream goes back

00:19:33.696 --> 00:19:36.412
to biological viability.

00:19:36.412 --> 00:19:38.177
It's not a 10 year thing,
it's not a 20 year thing,

00:19:38.177 --> 00:19:41.474
it's not a 50 year thing,
it's a tomorrow kinda thing.

00:19:41.474 --> 00:19:44.493
Artists are not people who
create from themselves,

00:19:44.493 --> 00:19:46.030
but they actually serve a roll

00:19:46.030 --> 00:19:49.115
as communicators for the
divine in the universe.

00:19:49.115 --> 00:19:51.844
I'm always thinking about these
colors and how they relate

00:19:51.844 --> 00:19:54.084
to where it came from.

00:19:54.084 --> 00:19:55.791
- [Announcer] Thanks to the teams efforts,

00:19:55.791 --> 00:19:59.634
in September of 2016, John
was invited to exhibit his art

00:19:59.634 --> 00:20:01.712
at the United Nations in New York City.

00:20:01.712 --> 00:20:04.406
- I'm very lucky to be
invited to have a show at the

00:20:04.406 --> 00:20:05.694
United Nations Headquarters.

00:20:05.694 --> 00:20:07.087
And what I'm really hoping to do

00:20:07.087 --> 00:20:09.235
is to form even more collaborations

00:20:09.235 --> 00:20:11.395
with people in many
places around the world,

00:20:11.395 --> 00:20:13.438
where we're all working towards this goal

00:20:13.438 --> 00:20:15.855
of a more sustainable planet.

00:20:17.444 --> 00:20:21.066
(light xylophone music)

00:20:21.066 --> 00:20:22.656
- [Announcer] In British Columbia,

00:20:22.656 --> 00:20:24.978
Galyn Franklin spends
much of his free time

00:20:24.978 --> 00:20:26.975
free diving with sea lions.

00:20:26.975 --> 00:20:29.808
And it's safe to say, he loves it.

00:20:32.037 --> 00:20:36.238
Each winter, Franklin heads
to nearby Hornby Island,

00:20:36.238 --> 00:20:38.031
to play with the curious beasts

00:20:38.031 --> 00:20:40.469
who gather to feed on the herring,

00:20:40.469 --> 00:20:43.581
that span in the cold
waters, 40 feet below.

00:20:43.581 --> 00:20:46.581
(light piano music)

00:20:48.863 --> 00:20:50.535
(sea lions barking)

00:20:50.535 --> 00:20:54.529
- I kind of compare it with
being at a giant dog park.

00:20:54.529 --> 00:20:56.073
Some of them love it,
like you start giving them

00:20:56.073 --> 00:20:59.140
armpit scratches, and
they're just like giant dogs,

00:20:59.140 --> 00:21:00.603
they just start rollin' around

00:21:00.603 --> 00:21:01.985
and they're flyin' all around your,

00:21:01.985 --> 00:21:03.831
but these animals are huge.

00:21:03.831 --> 00:21:04.969
People are intimidated by them,

00:21:04.969 --> 00:21:07.174
but once you learn their behavior,

00:21:07.174 --> 00:21:08.475
they're just right into you, right away.

00:21:08.475 --> 00:21:11.216
They just look at you like a big chew toy.

00:21:11.216 --> 00:21:15.362
(lively xylophone music)

00:21:15.362 --> 00:21:16.959
(engine starting)

00:21:16.959 --> 00:21:18.822
(piano music)

00:21:18.822 --> 00:21:21.572
(engine purring)

00:21:26.706 --> 00:21:27.539
I could never see myself

00:21:27.539 --> 00:21:30.827
living anywhere other than
the Pacific Northwest.

00:21:30.827 --> 00:21:32.696
I could literally spend every single day

00:21:32.696 --> 00:21:33.556
of the rest of my life

00:21:33.556 --> 00:21:35.924
and never travel anywhere
other than Vancouver Island,

00:21:35.924 --> 00:21:37.271
and I would still never have enough time

00:21:37.271 --> 00:21:39.662
to see this whole place.

00:21:39.662 --> 00:21:41.090
My first introduction to the ocean

00:21:41.090 --> 00:21:43.331
would have definitely come from my mom.

00:21:43.331 --> 00:21:45.328
That's me with the sea lion right there.

00:21:45.328 --> 00:21:48.103
Later on in life, realize it
is a pretty unique experience

00:21:48.103 --> 00:21:49.227
to get to go and spend time

00:21:49.227 --> 00:21:52.677
with these animals on a regular basis.

00:21:52.677 --> 00:21:55.905
Sea lions are a large, intelligent animal

00:21:55.905 --> 00:21:59.647
and they're very sensitive
of what your intentions are,

00:21:59.647 --> 00:22:01.691
and how they choose to interact with you

00:22:01.691 --> 00:22:03.397
is totally their choice.

00:22:03.397 --> 00:22:05.685
But if you're comfortable
and they’re inquisitive,

00:22:05.685 --> 00:22:07.240
they'll come and they'll chew on your fins

00:22:07.240 --> 00:22:09.841
or they come up and they chew
on the edge of your mask,

00:22:09.841 --> 00:22:12.616
or give you big kisses
on the lips, or big hugs,

00:22:12.616 --> 00:22:16.783
they wrap their fins around
you and give you a big hug.

00:22:19.245 --> 00:22:22.136
People get sketched out about it.

00:22:22.136 --> 00:22:23.924
Like I've had lots of people
contact me and be like,

00:22:23.924 --> 00:22:25.874
"Don't you think those
things are aggressive?"

00:22:25.874 --> 00:22:28.127
Just because an animal is
putting it's mouth on you

00:22:28.127 --> 00:22:30.646
doesn't mean it's doing
it in a harmful way.

00:22:30.646 --> 00:22:33.386
They don't have hands, they
can't explore their world

00:22:33.386 --> 00:22:36.469
with anything other than their mouth.

00:22:37.964 --> 00:22:42.131
(peaceful music &amp; gently bubbling water)

00:22:47.132 --> 00:22:49.385
There's people that spend a lot of money

00:22:49.385 --> 00:22:50.894
to travel to the other side of the planet

00:22:50.894 --> 00:22:53.634
to watch a group of large
migratory animals in Africa,

00:22:53.634 --> 00:22:56.778
or something, and here I
can just go out on my boat and,

00:22:56.778 --> 00:22:59.436
it's not something I take
for granted at all anymore,

00:22:59.436 --> 00:23:02.281
having the opportunity to do this.

00:23:02.281 --> 00:23:05.031
(peaceful music)

00:23:08.887 --> 00:23:11.970
(gentle piano music)

00:23:15.556 --> 00:23:18.639
(light rain falling0

00:23:19.736 --> 00:23:21.477
- In the Netherlands it rains a lot.

00:23:21.477 --> 00:23:24.577
Since I grew up, the
weather is really changing.

00:23:24.577 --> 00:23:26.864
It rains more, it rains more heavy,

00:23:26.864 --> 00:23:29.558
and it's getting warmer as well.

00:23:29.558 --> 00:23:33.354
People have a tendency to
complain about the weather.

00:23:33.354 --> 00:23:36.048
I think it's beautiful
when you hear the rain.

00:23:36.048 --> 00:23:39.844
It really changes space,
and it also sustains growth.

00:23:39.844 --> 00:23:43.188
I wanted to make something
to celebrate the weather.

00:23:43.188 --> 00:23:46.605
(light, symphonic music)

00:23:50.270 --> 00:23:53.103
(symphonic music)

00:23:55.610 --> 00:23:56.443
Made By Rain,

00:23:56.443 --> 00:23:59.883
started after I got the
heritage of my grandfather.

00:23:59.883 --> 00:24:03.215
This heritage was a
collection of calendars,

00:24:03.215 --> 00:24:06.559
where he wrote down, every
day, the weather conditions.

00:24:06.559 --> 00:24:08.265
When I was doing my Masters,

00:24:08.265 --> 00:24:12.189
I was thinking like, "How can
I actually map the weather?"

00:24:12.189 --> 00:24:14.511
And then I started to doing
the same as my grandfather

00:24:14.511 --> 00:24:15.986
but then with textiles.

00:24:15.986 --> 00:24:19.004
I made this technique Pluviography,

00:24:19.004 --> 00:24:21.524
which is drawing with rain.

00:24:21.524 --> 00:24:25.191
(deep bass symphonic music)

00:24:27.092 --> 00:24:29.948
There are two ways to make
Made By Rain textiles.

00:24:29.948 --> 00:24:33.129
One I call analog version
and one the digital version.

00:24:33.129 --> 00:24:36.635
When I am in the Hague, at my
studio and it starts to rain,

00:24:36.635 --> 00:24:40.049
I go up on the roof and
for the analog version

00:24:40.049 --> 00:24:44.216
I use two layers of textile
and of ink with paper,

00:24:45.552 --> 00:24:49.220
and when the rain hits the
surface, the paper gets wet

00:24:49.220 --> 00:24:50.486
and it starts to bleed,

00:24:50.486 --> 00:24:51.949
so that makes that the white cloth

00:24:51.949 --> 00:24:55.084
becomes black or blue or
depending on the color.

00:24:55.084 --> 00:24:59.890
The digital version is
prepared with the layer of ink

00:24:59.890 --> 00:25:03.361
already on the cloth, and
when the rain hits the surface

00:25:03.361 --> 00:25:05.361
the ink starts to bleed.

00:25:06.741 --> 00:25:10.408
(deep bass symphonic music) 

00:25:12.258 --> 00:25:14.115
After I put it in the rain,

00:25:14.115 --> 00:25:18.399
I fixate the textiles
and then you can wear it.

00:25:18.399 --> 00:25:20.605
With this technique you
can see every raindrop

00:25:20.605 --> 00:25:23.995
and every little detail in the rain.

00:25:23.995 --> 00:25:26.828
(xylophone music)

00:25:28.441 --> 00:25:31.623
So after I fixate my textiles,

00:25:31.623 --> 00:25:34.850
I check the data from the weather station

00:25:34.850 --> 00:25:39.587
in order to really finalize
the moment and the data,

00:25:39.587 --> 00:25:42.849
which is silk-screened upon the textile.

00:25:42.849 --> 00:25:45.380
Weather data comes in statistics,

00:25:45.380 --> 00:25:50.141
and I wanted to make a more
visual way of archiving weather.

00:25:50.141 --> 00:25:53.891
On the textiles I note
the location, the date,

00:25:53.891 --> 00:25:57.234
the time interval, the
millimeters of rain,

00:25:57.234 --> 00:25:59.846
and the weather circumstances.

00:25:59.846 --> 00:26:03.028
And I do this because I see
the textiles as documents,

00:26:03.028 --> 00:26:04.049
so they're all unique.

00:26:04.049 --> 00:26:06.545
Five minutes of drizzle
is very different than

00:26:06.545 --> 00:26:09.120
five minutes of heavy rainfall.

00:26:09.120 --> 00:26:13.539
(tinkling piano music
&amp; light rain falling)

00:26:13.539 --> 00:26:17.858
The book Made By Rain, it's in
three parts, combined in one.

00:26:17.858 --> 00:26:20.958
One part is the research,
one part is fabrics,

00:26:20.958 --> 00:26:23.373
and one part is an artistic interpretation

00:26:23.373 --> 00:26:25.730
of how the textiles can be applied.

00:26:25.730 --> 00:26:28.911
I'm very fascinated by
water in all kinds of forms.

00:26:28.911 --> 00:26:30.374
The fact that water is able

00:26:30.374 --> 00:26:33.439
to transform between
solid, gas, and liquids

00:26:33.439 --> 00:26:36.864
brings for me a lot of new
materials to investigate,

00:26:36.864 --> 00:26:38.535
and to use in my work.

00:26:38.535 --> 00:26:42.702
I traveled all around the
country, already, to map the rain.

00:26:43.586 --> 00:26:45.826
I've also been in Japan and in Beijing,

00:26:45.826 --> 00:26:48.148
so my dream is to make a Rain Atlas.

00:26:48.148 --> 00:26:52.315
(light piano music &amp;
sewing machine whirring)

00:26:53.640 --> 00:26:56.055
I've hoped that with the
textiles that I make,

00:26:56.055 --> 00:26:59.689
people get a new awareness of rain,

00:26:59.689 --> 00:27:02.673
and a new relationship
with the environment.

00:27:02.673 --> 00:27:05.436
I hope people see the beauty as well,

00:27:05.436 --> 00:27:07.630
not only the pouring rain,

00:27:07.630 --> 00:27:11.797
and waiting for it to be dry
to continue your daily life.

00:27:14.833 --> 00:27:16.704
(light violin music)

00:27:16.704 --> 00:27:19.560
- [Announcer] In the fertile
hills of Derbyshire, England,

00:27:19.560 --> 00:27:23.196
one man is cultivating something unique.

00:27:23.196 --> 00:27:26.196
- I'm Gavin Monro, I grow furniture.

00:27:30.252 --> 00:27:32.468
(plucky sympnonic music)

00:27:32.468 --> 00:27:34.701
This is our furniture orchard.

00:27:34.701 --> 00:27:38.789
We've got chairs this
side, lamps over here,

00:27:38.789 --> 00:27:41.355
tables little further around that way,

00:27:41.355 --> 00:27:44.014
and all manner of things in between.

00:27:44.014 --> 00:27:46.870
The original idea came from realizing

00:27:46.870 --> 00:27:49.238
that the way that we make furniture now

00:27:49.238 --> 00:27:53.221
is that we have to grow the
trees for at least 50 years,

00:27:53.221 --> 00:27:55.218
and then we cut them down
and we make them into

00:27:55.218 --> 00:27:57.632
smaller and smaller and smaller bits,

00:27:57.632 --> 00:27:59.745
before sticking them back together again.

00:27:59.745 --> 00:28:02.230
Why bother with that at all?

00:28:02.230 --> 00:28:03.577
- [Announcer] Why indeed?

00:28:03.577 --> 00:28:06.061
Gavin trains the branches of young trees

00:28:06.061 --> 00:28:08.476
by attaching them to specially made forms.

00:28:08.476 --> 00:28:12.064
He then grafts them together
to form one solid piece.

00:28:12.064 --> 00:28:15.814
Slowly they grow into
fully-formed pieces of furniture.

00:28:15.814 --> 00:28:18.054
But exactly how slow are we talking?

00:28:18.054 --> 00:28:21.282
- For a chair to grow, that
takes five to 10 years,

00:28:21.282 --> 00:28:22.826
depending on the species.

00:28:22.826 --> 00:28:23.659
The thing is,

00:28:23.659 --> 00:28:26.332
if you're thinking about
this in the bigger picture,

00:28:26.332 --> 00:28:29.560
then by the time you've
grown your tree for 50 years,

00:28:29.560 --> 00:28:33.727
we'll have at least 10
generations of chairs rolled out.

00:28:34.773 --> 00:28:35.911
While it seems slow,

00:28:35.911 --> 00:28:38.151
in real terms of making
things out of wood,

00:28:38.151 --> 00:28:40.601
it's actually pretty quick.

00:28:40.601 --> 00:28:43.608
(sawing &amp; violin music)

00:28:43.608 --> 00:28:45.361
When we harvest the trees,

00:28:45.361 --> 00:28:46.917
we're not killing them,

00:28:46.917 --> 00:28:48.101
the varieties that we use

00:28:48.101 --> 00:28:50.585
all grow back again the next year.

00:28:50.585 --> 00:28:53.116
On the environmental front,
it's not just efficient,

00:28:53.116 --> 00:28:54.869
it's uber efficient.

00:28:54.869 --> 00:28:56.587
We'll, we've only waited
10 years for that.

00:28:56.587 --> 00:28:59.675
(both men laughing)

00:28:59.675 --> 00:29:01.835
We've got this lovely
curve coming up here.

00:29:01.835 --> 00:29:04.203
When we sand this down,

00:29:04.203 --> 00:29:07.431
this little triangle's
gonna look gorgeous.

00:29:07.431 --> 00:29:09.881
- You got it, do you?

00:29:09.881 --> 00:29:11.390
- We can get them into the workshop

00:29:11.390 --> 00:29:14.525
and the fun begins on the production side

00:29:14.525 --> 00:29:17.624
and we can start to kind of bring together

00:29:17.624 --> 00:29:19.494
geometry and nature.

00:29:19.494 --> 00:29:23.406
The finishing is where we
can really let that shine.

00:29:23.406 --> 00:29:24.300
- [Announcer] And no one can deny

00:29:24.300 --> 00:29:28.491
that Gavin's furniture is
beautiful, but is it functional?

00:29:28.491 --> 00:29:32.578
- Not only do we think
that they'll be stronger,

00:29:32.578 --> 00:29:36.282
they'll be way stronger because
they're one solid piece,

00:29:36.282 --> 00:29:38.604
there's no joints to come loose.

00:29:38.604 --> 00:29:41.750
What we're doing here is
collaborating with the trees.

00:29:41.750 --> 00:29:44.594
It's kind of a beautiful
way of working, really.

00:29:44.594 --> 00:29:47.761
(plucky violin music)

00:29:50.075 --> 00:29:53.679
(harmonic vibrating)

00:29:53.679 --> 00:29:57.846
- The weather is really in
your face in New Orleans.

00:29:58.938 --> 00:30:02.735
It is a character in the
play of New Orleans.

00:30:02.735 --> 00:30:04.604
- [Announcer] In the ninth ward,

00:30:04.604 --> 00:30:07.170
inventor and musician QUINTRON,

00:30:07.170 --> 00:30:09.910
is fueled by a desire to
hear things differently.

00:30:09.910 --> 00:30:14.228
That's why he built a device
that turns weather into music.

00:30:14.228 --> 00:30:15.668
- This instrument, the Weather Warlock,

00:30:15.668 --> 00:30:19.418
is an audio mirror of
the sunrise and sunset.

00:30:20.324 --> 00:30:21.787
- [Announcer] The Weather
Warlock uses sensors

00:30:21.787 --> 00:30:24.968
to detect changes in light, precipitation,

00:30:24.968 --> 00:30:29.496
and wind, and transforms
them into a haunting drone.

00:30:29.496 --> 00:30:31.945
(haunting drone music)

00:30:31.945 --> 00:30:33.373
Which he streams to anyone

00:30:33.373 --> 00:30:35.695
wanting to rock out to Mother Nature.

00:30:35.695 --> 00:30:38.679
(haunting drone music)

00:30:38.679 --> 00:30:40.827
- My name is QUINTRON,
it's not my real name,

00:30:40.827 --> 00:30:43.277
it's a given, musician’s name.

00:30:43.277 --> 00:30:45.808
I definitely consider
myself first and foremost

00:30:45.808 --> 00:30:48.710
to be a musician, but I'm also a builder,

00:30:48.710 --> 00:30:52.019
and inventor of electronic
musical instruments

00:30:52.019 --> 00:30:54.457
and the device that
we're talking about today

00:30:54.457 --> 00:30:56.164
is called the Weather Warlock,

00:30:56.164 --> 00:30:59.747
which is a weather-
controlled analog synth.

00:31:02.619 --> 00:31:06.125
It's using the weather to create music.

00:31:06.125 --> 00:31:10.292
There's no microphones,
it's not the sound of rain.

00:31:11.187 --> 00:31:13.962
That puttering sound is
the sound of that sensor

00:31:13.962 --> 00:31:18.129
just starting to get enough
light to sonically activate it.

00:31:19.175 --> 00:31:22.425
(throbbing vibrations)

00:31:23.378 --> 00:31:25.096
- [Announcer] The Weather
Warlock’s outdoor sensors

00:31:25.096 --> 00:31:27.337
detect atmospheric changes.

00:31:27.337 --> 00:31:29.705
- There is a temperature sensor,

00:31:29.705 --> 00:31:33.872
there is a set of our wind
speed controlled sensors,

00:31:35.231 --> 00:31:39.980
there is a moisture sensor,
or rain, or humidity, or snow,

00:31:39.980 --> 00:31:42.186
and the last one would be the sky sensor,

00:31:42.186 --> 00:31:45.169
which is a sensor at the
top, on a little periscope

00:31:45.169 --> 00:31:47.252
that's aiming at the sky.

00:31:49.163 --> 00:31:50.092
- [Announcer] Wires then carry

00:31:50.092 --> 00:31:51.973
all the collected data into the synth,

00:31:51.973 --> 00:31:56.245
which converts the information into sound.

00:31:56.245 --> 00:31:58.242
- So you can play it
like an instrument too.

00:31:58.242 --> 00:31:59.543
You can just let it do it's thing,

00:31:59.543 --> 00:32:03.293
but I can also change
the timbre of the drum.

00:32:05.580 --> 00:32:09.249
Usually the most action is
during sunrise and sunset.

00:32:09.249 --> 00:32:12.465
It's the closest thing
to an audio soundtrack

00:32:12.465 --> 00:32:14.508
because it's moving at the same speed.

00:32:14.508 --> 00:32:18.675
It's literally an audio mirror
of what you're looking at.

00:32:20.568 --> 00:32:22.937
So sunrise is official over,

00:32:22.937 --> 00:32:25.687
according to the Weather Warlock.

00:32:27.561 --> 00:32:29.714
And everyone else.

00:32:29.714 --> 00:32:32.964
(haunting drone music)

00:32:45.596 --> 00:32:46.746
Check, one two.

00:32:46.746 --> 00:32:51.401
Good evening, you are tuned
in to weatherfortheblind.org,

00:32:51.401 --> 00:32:55.792
broadcasting as usual out
of New Orleans, Louisiana.

00:32:55.792 --> 00:32:57.042
The Weather Warlock,

00:32:57.042 --> 00:33:01.209
has become a live, 24/7
online musical feed.

00:33:03.250 --> 00:33:04.434
- [Announcer] The livestream is called

00:33:04.434 --> 00:33:05.943
Weather For The Blind.

00:33:05.943 --> 00:33:09.090
Figuratively referring to
the circadian rhythm disorder

00:33:09.090 --> 00:33:11.539
sometimes experienced by blind people,

00:33:11.539 --> 00:33:15.622
who don't visually experience
sunrise and sunset.

00:33:17.472 --> 00:33:20.300
- Sunrise was at 5:15 this morning,

00:33:20.300 --> 00:33:23.717
and sunset happening now as you can hear,

00:33:25.721 --> 00:33:28.054
according to our UV sensors.

00:33:30.122 --> 00:33:31.062
Every moment of the day

00:33:31.062 --> 00:33:33.953
there's something in flux in the weather,

00:33:33.953 --> 00:33:38.036
and to capture that in
audio became my intention.

00:33:40.780 --> 00:33:43.508
Our ears are always there,

00:33:43.508 --> 00:33:48.129
those of us that are fortunate
enough to be able to hear,

00:33:48.129 --> 00:33:52.482
that's a powerful funnel
to the soul and the brain.

00:33:52.482 --> 00:33:55.565
(light, funky music)

00:34:02.664 --> 00:34:05.414
(tinkling bells)

00:34:09.723 --> 00:34:11.592
- [Announcer] Deep in the desert of Utah,

00:34:11.592 --> 00:34:15.110
thousands of rock
formations known as Hoodoos

00:34:15.110 --> 00:34:17.386
stand guard over the barren landscape

00:34:17.386 --> 00:34:19.719
of Goblin Valley State Park.

00:34:21.020 --> 00:34:24.596
Hoodoos are mushroom-shaped
sandstone, and Goblin Valley

00:34:24.596 --> 00:34:29.042
has the highest occurrence
of them in the world.

00:34:29.042 --> 00:34:31.817
Typically found in dry, rocky areas,

00:34:31.817 --> 00:34:35.439
these unusual shapes are the
result of wind, and water,

00:34:35.439 --> 00:34:38.748
breaking down the soft sandstone.

00:34:38.748 --> 00:34:41.360
Rainwater removes the weaker rock.

00:34:41.360 --> 00:34:43.276
Windblown sands and dust

00:34:43.276 --> 00:34:47.485
carve the stone into
eerie goblin silhouettes.

00:34:47.485 --> 00:34:51.235
(pulsating light rock music)

00:34:54.340 --> 00:34:57.475
Other vegetation rarely
inhabits Goblin Valley,

00:34:57.475 --> 00:35:01.642
due to the lack of water
and sweltering conditions.

00:35:04.688 --> 00:35:07.902
Goblin Valley has often
been compared to Mars.

00:35:07.902 --> 00:35:10.652
(tinkling bells)

00:35:14.102 --> 00:35:18.177
(action symphonic music)

00:35:18.177 --> 00:35:19.024
- [Announcer] Scientists say

00:35:19.024 --> 00:35:21.881
that Joshua trees are disappearing.

00:35:21.881 --> 00:35:24.052
- At low elevation and in the south,

00:35:24.052 --> 00:35:26.002
Joshua trees are not making it.

00:35:26.002 --> 00:35:28.878
- [Announcer] Across the
Southwestern Mojave Desert.

00:35:28.878 --> 00:35:29.819
- [Professor Smith] The only places

00:35:29.819 --> 00:35:31.094
where we find seedlings,

00:35:31.094 --> 00:35:33.920
are up at higher elevation, like here.

00:35:33.920 --> 00:35:35.917
They're facing increasing

00:35:35.917 --> 00:35:37.705
and new threats to their existence.

00:35:37.705 --> 00:35:40.967
The combination of increasing
temperature and drought,

00:35:40.967 --> 00:35:44.392
alongside increasing frequency of fire.

00:35:44.392 --> 00:35:46.227
- One lightning strike in a dry storm

00:35:46.227 --> 00:35:48.874
and the whole thing goes
poof, and that's not good.

00:35:48.874 --> 00:35:51.440
- [Announcer] Professor
Chris Smith and his team

00:35:51.440 --> 00:35:52.740
are on a multi-year mission

00:35:52.740 --> 00:35:55.143
to map the genome of Joshua trees,

00:35:55.143 --> 00:35:59.311
in the hopes of saving the
species from total eradication.

00:35:59.311 --> 00:36:02.005
- And the idea of losing that species,

00:36:02.005 --> 00:36:04.733
affects people in a really personal way.

00:36:04.733 --> 00:36:06.892
- [Announcer] By unlocking
the plants' genetic code,

00:36:06.892 --> 00:36:09.748
they hope to reveal it's
best chance for survival

00:36:09.748 --> 00:36:12.930
in the face of rising
temperatures in the west.

00:36:12.930 --> 00:36:16.097
(action violin music)

00:36:18.750 --> 00:36:21.417
(western music)

00:36:22.979 --> 00:36:24.326
- [Professor Smith] According to legend,

00:36:24.326 --> 00:36:26.857
Mormon settlers saw in Joshua trees

00:36:26.857 --> 00:36:29.260
the image of the Prophet Joshua,

00:36:29.260 --> 00:36:31.095
pointing the way towards
the Promised Land.

00:36:31.095 --> 00:36:32.395
- Joshua trees are odd looking,

00:36:32.395 --> 00:36:34.508
with their branches that
stick up in all directions

00:36:34.508 --> 00:36:36.505
with spines sticking up towards the sky.

00:36:36.505 --> 00:36:38.956
- [Professor Smith] They're
technically a kind of Yucca,

00:36:38.956 --> 00:36:42.706
some people call them
the Dr. Seuss plant.

00:36:45.687 --> 00:36:49.020
(twanging guitar music)

00:36:51.727 --> 00:36:54.954
They are this important
symbol of Mojave Desert,

00:36:54.954 --> 00:36:57.785
emblematic of the people
that live in the desert.

00:36:57.785 --> 00:37:00.815
And the idea of losing that species

00:37:00.815 --> 00:37:03.253
affects people in a really personal way.

00:37:03.253 --> 00:37:06.179
- They're many other organisms
that depend on Joshua trees

00:37:06.179 --> 00:37:08.373
for food and habitat, and shelter,

00:37:08.373 --> 00:37:10.057
and if Joshua trees go extinct,

00:37:10.057 --> 00:37:12.994
those other organisms are also doomed.

00:37:12.994 --> 00:37:16.597
If we could identify the
exact genes that are involved

00:37:16.597 --> 00:37:18.328
in adaptation to climate change,

00:37:18.328 --> 00:37:20.534
potentially we could breed Joshua trees

00:37:20.534 --> 00:37:22.403
that could survive into the future,

00:37:22.403 --> 00:37:25.601
even as the climate changes around them.

00:37:25.601 --> 00:37:30.113
(light, dramatic piano music)

00:37:30.113 --> 00:37:31.852
- [Professor Smith] We've
been able to bring together

00:37:31.852 --> 00:37:35.116
an enormous number of
people who are cooperating.

00:37:35.116 --> 00:37:38.216
We have ecologists, we
have genetic scientists,

00:37:38.216 --> 00:37:39.760
we have government agencies,

00:37:39.760 --> 00:37:41.560
and we even have citizen scientists,

00:37:41.560 --> 00:37:45.060
all coming together to solve this problem.

00:37:51.765 --> 00:37:53.916
- So we're gonna measure
the width of the leaves.

00:37:53.916 --> 00:37:55.332
We measure from the mid-point

00:37:55.332 --> 00:37:57.991
and you read it by looking
at where the zero lines up,

00:37:57.991 --> 00:38:00.894
which is about 11.4 millimeters.

00:38:00.894 --> 00:38:02.890
- When you think about all of
the things that it requires

00:38:02.890 --> 00:38:05.572
for a Joshua tree to
actually make it from a seed

00:38:05.572 --> 00:38:07.407
to an adult plant,

00:38:07.407 --> 00:38:10.797
the challenges that they
face seem insurmountable.

00:38:10.797 --> 00:38:14.536
Each fruit contains a
hundred to 200 seeds,

00:38:14.536 --> 00:38:17.523
maybe one of those
might ultimately make it

00:38:17.523 --> 00:38:20.106
to grow up to be a Joshua tree.

00:38:21.829 --> 00:38:25.662
(light, dramatic piano music)

00:38:26.857 --> 00:38:28.401
We're collecting leaf tissue,

00:38:28.401 --> 00:38:31.048
and that tissue we're
gonna take back to the lab

00:38:31.048 --> 00:38:32.593
and extract DNA,

00:38:32.593 --> 00:38:35.170
and by comparing different
parts of the desert,

00:38:35.170 --> 00:38:36.958
we can start to identify genes

00:38:36.958 --> 00:38:41.894
that are involved in
adaptation to climate change.

00:38:41.894 --> 00:38:44.561
(melodic music)

00:38:50.460 --> 00:38:51.970
- When we get the material from the field,

00:38:51.970 --> 00:38:53.850
we grind it up in liquid nitrogen

00:38:53.850 --> 00:38:57.554
and liberate the DNA from the
rest of the cell material.

00:38:57.554 --> 00:39:01.966
After extracting the DNA, we're
sequencing it in small bits

00:39:01.966 --> 00:39:04.566
and trying to stitch those pieces together

00:39:04.566 --> 00:39:05.948
just like putting together

00:39:05.948 --> 00:39:08.769
a very complicated, massive puzzle.

00:39:08.769 --> 00:39:12.368
The genome really offers us a
blueprint of the Joshua tree

00:39:12.368 --> 00:39:13.262
to better understand

00:39:13.262 --> 00:39:16.072
which genes might be allowing it to live

00:39:16.072 --> 00:39:18.150
under drier or hotter conditions.

00:39:18.150 --> 00:39:20.507
What we need to do is choose individuals

00:39:20.507 --> 00:39:22.585
that seem to be best able to deal

00:39:22.585 --> 00:39:26.752
with increasing temperatures
or reduced water availability.

00:39:27.647 --> 00:39:30.213
Those are the ones that we'll
focus on trying to propagate

00:39:30.213 --> 00:39:31.885
in face of this changing climate.

00:39:31.885 --> 00:39:33.885
- This is the pure gold.

00:39:34.850 --> 00:39:36.816
This is how we're gonna connect back

00:39:36.816 --> 00:39:38.859
the patterns that we're
seeing on the landscape,

00:39:38.859 --> 00:39:41.425
to the genetic data
into the genome project.

00:39:41.425 --> 00:39:44.445
This is the most important part.

00:39:44.445 --> 00:39:48.925
(gently strumming guitar music)

00:39:48.925 --> 00:39:49.947
- [Dr. Esque] We're right in the middle

00:39:49.947 --> 00:39:51.282
of what was a fairly large fire,

00:39:51.282 --> 00:39:53.279
- I'm really quite shocked, honestly,

00:39:53.279 --> 00:39:55.566
to see how many small trees there are.

00:39:55.566 --> 00:39:56.932
- [Dr. Esque] We've got a
lot of really good recovery

00:39:56.932 --> 00:39:58.468
in this old burned area.

00:39:58.468 --> 00:39:59.850
It's pretty exciting to see it,

00:39:59.850 --> 00:40:00.759
it really makes you feel good

00:40:00.759 --> 00:40:02.207
when you see something like this.

00:40:02.207 --> 00:40:05.028
- You can see many young
Joshuas, a foot and a half high,

00:40:05.028 --> 00:40:08.093
maybe smaller, which is a
sign that the populations

00:40:08.093 --> 00:40:09.962
are doing fine here.

00:40:09.962 --> 00:40:12.458
I can't imagine the desert without them.

00:40:12.458 --> 00:40:16.568
So I hope they're doing as well
as they look like they are.

00:40:16.568 --> 00:40:17.938
- I definitely have days

00:40:17.938 --> 00:40:20.876
when I've been walking
around for 12 hours,

00:40:20.876 --> 00:40:23.325
lugging a ladder through the desert

00:40:23.325 --> 00:40:26.623
and I wonder why the hell I'm doing this.

00:40:26.623 --> 00:40:28.620
What we saw today, gives me hope.

00:40:28.620 --> 00:40:31.220
Joshua trees re-sprouting
from root stalks,

00:40:31.220 --> 00:40:33.345
and it makes me think,
"Okay, it is possible

00:40:33.345 --> 00:40:35.586
that we could figure
out ways that organisms

00:40:35.586 --> 00:40:36.897
could adapt to climate change."

00:40:36.897 --> 00:40:37.989
That's kind of it for me.

00:40:37.989 --> 00:40:40.572
That's the dream of a lifetime.

00:40:44.872 --> 00:40:47.039
(pinging)

